<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<CheatTable CheatEngineTableVersion="45">
  <CheatEntries>
    <CheatEntry>
      <ID>100</ID>
      <Description>"README - how this table is built (read me first)"</Description>
      <Options moHideChildren="1"/>
      <LastState/>
      <VariableType>Byte</VariableType>
      <Address>0</Address>
      <Comments>"=====================================================================
 NARUTO MOBILE (com.tencent.KiHan) - MEMORY RESEARCH TABLE
 Read-only memory analysis. For study / reference only.
 Do NOT use in online matches. Do not use for anything illegal.
=====================================================================

WHAT THIS IS
  A Cheat Engine companion to the GitHub project "naruto-hp-overlay".
  Every mechanism that was actually verified is documented here as an
  entry + English comments. Entries that resolve to a value are real
  pointer chains, the rest are documentation-only rows.

ADDRESSING MODEL (important)
  Nothing is a fixed absolute address. Everything hangs off the module:

      libil2cpp.so + &lt;static root offset>     &lt;- stable per game version
        + off0 -> off1 -> ... -> offN         &lt;- pointer dereferences
        + &lt;field offset>                      &lt;- offset inside the object

  The absolute VA changes every time the game process restarts, the
  account changes, or the map changes. That is why the table uses the
  module + pointer-chain form instead of raw addresses.

OFFSET ORDER CONVENTION IN THIS FILE
  The &lt;Offsets> list is written in REVERSE evaluation order:
  the LAST &lt;Offset> element is applied FIRST to the module base.
  So this list:
        &lt;Offset>178&lt;/Offset> &lt;Offset>18&lt;/Offset> ... &lt;Offset>19C30&lt;/Offset>
  means:
        [[[...[libil2cpp.so+4913E20]+19C30]...]+18]+178
  Every entry repeats the full readable expression in its comments so
  you can rebuild the chain even if your CE version orders them
  differently.

HOW TO RE-FIND EVERYTHING AFTER A GAME UPDATE
  1) adb shell cat /proc/&lt;pid>/maps   -> confirm libil2cpp.so base
  2) scan for the HP block TAIL signature (see group 01)
  3) read the owner pointer at block+0x10 to pair the two copies
  4) rebuild the chain from the static root, or just use the
     --dump mode of the overlay binary, it does all of this.

PAIR DISTANCE
  Each entity stores its HP twice (value A = settled, value B = live).
  The distance between the two copies is ACCOUNT SPECIFIC.
  Sample config used here: self 0x56C0, enemy 0x36C0.
  Always re-measure, never hardcode."</Comments>
    </CheatEntry>

    <CheatEntry>
      <ID>110</ID>
      <Description>"01 - HP BLOCK (verified structure)"</Description>
      <Options moHideChildren="1"/>
      <LastState/>
      <VariableType>Byte</VariableType>
      <Address>0</Address>
      <Comments>"Both players' HP is stored twice, and both copies share the
same structure. Finding rule: scan the heap for the 24-byte TAIL,
then rewind 0x38 bytes and validate the header.

BLOCK LAYOUT (offsets from block base)
  +0x00  float     current HP ratio, 0.0 empty .. 1.0 full
  +0x04  byte[8]   eight zero bytes
  +0x0C  int32     constant 1
  +0x10  uint64    owner pointer (UI component that owns this bar)
  +0x18  byte[32]  padding
  +0x38  byte[24]  TAIL signature:
                   01 00 00 00  01 00 00 00  02 00 00 00
                   01 00 00 00  01 00 00 00  01 00 00 00

VALIDATION (all must hold)
  value in [0.0, 1.0]
  bytes +04..+0B are all zero
  int32 at +0C equals 1
  owner at +0x10 is a readable heap pointer
  HP may legitimately be 0 (defeated) - shape check must still pass.

OWNER PAIRING
  The two copies of one entity share the same owner value. Different
  entities may share an owner too, so pairing uses owner + distance:
  distance must fall in 0x100 .. 0x8000.

STALE BLOCK FILTER
  Blocks left over from the previous round have unique owners.
  Rule: keep an owner only if it appears at least twice in the hit set."</Comments>
    </CheatEntry>

    <CheatEntry>
