    <CheatEntry>
      <ID>130</ID>
      <Description>"03 - IL2CPP RUNTIME INTROSPECTION"</Description>
      <Options moHideChildren="1"/>
      <LastState/>
      <VariableType>Byte</VariableType>
      <Address>0</Address>
      <Comments>"The game ships libil2cpp.so with an ENCRYPTED global-metadata.dat
header, so the classic Il2CppDumper route fails:

  on disk : 94 43 72 12 18 4B 9E EC 66 67 66 66 ...
  normal  : AF 1B B1 FA (magic) 1F 00 00 00 (version)

The magic is wrong, the version field is garbage, and a 56 GB scan of
the running process found no plaintext AF 1B B1 FA either (decryption
is done per block or the header is patched). Only the header is
encrypted - the string blob stayed plaintext: "Assembly-CSharp" is at
file offset 0xB7F73.

So instead of parsing the metadata file, read the runtime classes:

  1. every managed object starts with Il2CppClass*   (8 bytes)
  2. Il2CppClass holds name / namespaze as char*
  3. Il2CppClass.fields is an array of FieldInfo, stride 0x20,
     and each FieldInfo directly contains the FIELD OFFSET

VERIFIED LAYOUT (this build)

  Il2CppClass
    +0x00  Il2CppImage*  image
    +0x10  char*         name
    +0x18  char*         namespaze
    +0x80  FieldInfo*    fields

  FieldInfo (stride 0x20)
    +0x00  char*         name
    +0x08  Il2CppType*   type      (points into 0x4000_xxxx_xxxx range)
    +0x10  Il2CppClass*  parent
    +0x18  int32         offset
    +0x1C  uint32        token

  The +0x80 offset was cross checked on System.String: its field table
  lists _stringLength at 0x10 and _firstChar at 0x14, which matches the
  real C# string layout exactly, so the table is trustworthy.

HOW THE BOOTSTRAP WORKS (no name known in advance)
  Managed string layout: klass, monitor(0), length@0x10, chars@0x14.
  Scan the rw heap for that shape, tally the klass values, and the most
  frequent one is System.String's class:

      72E5D027C1B0   528679 hits   &lt;- System.String
      000000000000    13984
      0000FFFFFFFF       84

  From there: klass -> image -> types -> every class in the assembly.

CAVEAT
  The class table is rebuilt on every restart. A klass address that was
  valid before the restart becomes an unreadable page afterwards. Never
  hardcode a klass pointer in a table you want to keep."</Comments>
    </CheatEntry>

    <CheatEntry>
      <ID>131</ID>
      <Description>"System.String Il2CppClass* (example, session specific)"</Description>
      <VariableType>8 Bytes</VariableType>
      <Address>72E5D027C1B0</Address>
      <Comments>"This address only proves the mechanism; it will be dead after a
restart. Rebuild it with tools/find_strklass.py, then inspect with:

  python tools/ic.py 72E5D027C1B0
  -> klass 72E5D027C1B0  System.String
     fields @klass+0x80 (7 entries)
         _stringLength  off=0x0010
         _firstChar     off=0x0014"</Comments>
    </CheatEntry>

    <CheatEntry>
      <ID>140</ID>
      <Description>"04 - NOT FOUND: SKILL / SUMMON COOLDOWN (12 failed attempts)"</Description>
      <Options moHideChildren="1"/>
      <LastState/>
      <VariableType>Byte</VariableType>
      <Address>0</Address>
      <Comments>"Target: the remaining cooldown of skill 2. Known constraint: CD is
exactly 10 seconds and the button shows an integer countdown.
Result: not found. 12 methods, about 2.4 GB of readable heap, 0 hits,
while the screen was clearly showing a 7 second countdown.

EVERYTHING THAT WAS RULED OUT

 1. HP block +/-128KB
    Only particles and render matrices, 5 changing pages.

 2. Attribute record 0x240 itself
    Only +0x078 changed over 14 s, no countdown.

 3. HP heap region, +64MB, 3 Hz for 24 s
    Only nine 0&lt;->10 square waves: a round-end flag that clears for the
    whole region at once, not a countdown.

 4. GC heap 72E5C0000000-72E5D0000000, full 256 MB
    No 0..10 sawtooth.

 5. HUD root object (owner 72E4EB03B360) +/-8MB
    16 MB at 3 Hz for 30 s, nothing.

 6. Period alignment (strongest idea, still failed)
    CD is 10 s, so two samples 5 s apart must differ by exactly 5.0
    (0.5 for a 0..1 fillAmount). One pass covers the whole heap:
        5.0 s gap -> 26125 hits
        2.0 s gap -> 54206 hits
        intersection -> 479
    All 479 were 0&lt;->0.5 UI animations or garbage.

 7. Millisecond countdown (0..10000)
    Only 14 animation values, max 1704.

 8. fillAmount (0..1 ring mask)
    Note: an earlier filter required range >= 3 and silently killed all
    0..1 candidates. Fixed, rescanned the HUD region, still nothing.

 9. Pointer back-scan (--ptrscan, 2.4 GB in ~20 s)
    Holders of the HP block all live in 72E53B606xxx and become zero
    after 20 s: a per-frame render snapshot list, not a persistent
    object.

10. Two-level object tree from the attribute record
    19 pointers, 60 objects, 20 s sample: only 3 fields moved.

11. Full-heap widget enumeration (--widgets)
    Only 3 progress-bar widgets exist (two HP bars + one unknown),
    all constant at 1.0. The skill mask is not that widget class.

12. Remaining unmapped areas
    72E5F0000000 (compressed data, 190k noise hits), 72E580/72E648/
    72E670/72E6DC: zero hits.

FALSE POSITIVES WORTH REMEMBERING
    72E5C115xxx     recycled garbage
    72E4C0E008EC    4x4 transform matrix, stride 0x50
    72E4C017BD2C    5.000 -> 0.000 but actually -312149 garbage
    72E5C7E5BF34    graphics buffer garbage
    72E4C7CA45F8    graphics buffer, not the CD the user found by hand

CONCLUSION
  The cooldown is most likely NOT stored as a decreasing number. Two
  plausible models remain:
    a) endTime = global time + 10, computed on the fly (the four global
       clocks above support this; no 0..10 intermediate value exists)
    b) packed into a compressed struct or bitfield
  The remaining sane route is full il2cpp class enumeration:
  klass -> image -> types, filter names for Skill / CD / Cool, read the
  field offsets. tools/ic.py already does the first half."</Comments>
    </CheatEntry>

    <CheatEntry>
      <ID>150</ID>
      <Description>"05 - NON-MEMORY MECHANISMS (screen + performance)"</Description>
      <Options moHideChildren="1"/>
      <LastState/>
      <VariableType>Byte</VariableType>
      <Address>0</Address>
      <Comments>"Parts of the project that never touch memory. Documented here so
this file is a complete description of the tool.

BAR MEASUREMENT (pixels, not offsets)
  Early versions hardcoded the band at y in [0.106H, 0.124H] while the
  real bar sits at [0.077H, 0.088H], so every reading was zero. Now:
    1. FindBarBand: for each row in the right half, count "orange"
       pixels (R>90 AND R>1.5G AND R>1.4B). Consecutive rows above a
       threshold give the real top and bottom of the bar.
    2. Per row take the LONGEST CONTIGUOUS orange run, not the left and
       right extremes: the character portrait has a gold rim that is
       also orange and would double the width.
    3. Ratio = run width / (0.1765 * screen width). 0.1765 = 339/1920,
       the measured full-health width.
    4. Take the 70th percentile over all rows to reject outline and
       anti-aliasing rows.
    5. Write the resulting rect back to overlay.ini [view] so drawing and
       measuring share one source of truth.

OWNERSHIP CROSS CHECK
  Both entities look identical in memory, only owner and pair distance
  tell them apart, and the distance differs per account. So every 3 s
  the tool compares memory readings against the measured on-screen bars
  and swaps self/enemy if they are inverted, then persists the result.
  Two failed checks in a row invalidate the cache and trigger a rescan.

READ PERFORMANCE
  adb exec-out "toybox dd if=/proc/&lt;pid>/mem bs=4096 skip=&lt;page>"
    1 MB  52 ms | 4 MB 119 ms | 16 MB 277 ms  (~58 MB/s per channel)
  16 MB slices + 8 threads + guest gzip -1 + host zlib inflate
    -> ~200 MB/s aggregate, full 2.6 GB scan in ~19 s
  main thread aggregates every 120 ms and stops everyone on first hit
    -> typically 2-4 s
  cached start -> 0.4 s

GOTCHAS
  adb exec-out does not run a shell, so the whole pipeline must be one
  quoted argument. Never put 2>/dev/null inside: dd treats it as an
  argument and fails with "bad arg" - open a separate stderr pipe on
  the host instead.
  toybox gzip silently truncates input above 32 MB, so slices are 16 MB.
  miniz tinfl only reaches 70 MB/s and became the bottleneck; switched
  to zlib 1.3.1 inflate (300 MB/s+, only inflate sources are vendored)."</Comments>
    </CheatEntry>
  </CheatEntries>
</CheatTable>
